WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.755
IEC 62443 distributes cybersecurity responsibility across the industrial lifecycle.

00:00:05.755 --> 00:00:09.800
The asset owner owns or operates the industrial environment and remains accountable for

00:00:09.800 --> 00:00:11.225
understanding operational risk,

00:00:11.225 --> 00:00:13.955
defining security requirements, approving residual risk,

00:00:13.955 --> 00:00:16.200
and sustaining the security program.

00:00:16.200 --> 00:00:19.935
System integrators and automation solution providers turn requirements into a working

00:00:19.935 --> 00:00:20.670
architecture.

00:00:20.670 --> 00:00:23.600
They select and configure components, define zones and conduits,

00:00:23.600 --> 00:00:28.080
establish accounts and communication paths, test controls, and deliver documentation.

00:00:28.080 --> 00:00:32.280
Their work must satisfy both cybersecurity objectives and process requirements.

00:00:32.280 --> 00:00:35.760
Product suppliers develop hardware and software used in industrial systems.

00:00:35.760 --> 00:00:39.360
They are responsible for secure development practices, documented capabilities,

00:00:39.360 --> 00:00:41.535
vulnerability handling, update information,

00:00:41.535 --> 00:00:44.240
and clear assumptions about how a product should be deployed.

00:00:44.240 --> 00:00:48.540
A product's capability is useful only when the system design uses it correctly.

00:00:48.540 --> 00:00:52.125
Service and maintenance providers preserve or modify the system after commissioning.

00:00:52.125 --> 00:00:55.050
Their remote access, tools, credentials, change practices,

00:00:55.050 --> 00:00:58.665
and incident communication become part of the security architecture.

00:00:58.665 --> 00:01:01.740
Contracts and operating procedures should define what access is permitted,

00:01:01.740 --> 00:01:04.500
how it is approved, and what evidence must be retained.

00:01:04.500 --> 00:01:07.910
Shared responsibility does not mean vague responsibility.

00:01:07.910 --> 00:01:11.680
Projects should assign ownership for risk decisions, configuration, accounts, patching,

00:01:11.680 --> 00:01:16.000
backups, monitoring, incident response, documentation, and end-of-life planning.

00:01:16.000 --> 00:01:19.880
Handoffs between suppliers, integrators, and operators are common failure points.

00:01:19.880 --> 00:01:23.755
Acceptance criteria should verify that required controls work and that the asset owner

00:01:23.755 --> 00:01:26.288
receives the information needed to operate them.

00:01:26.288 --> 00:01:29.800
Security is strongest when every role understands both its obligations and its

00:01:29.800 --> 00:01:30.590
dependencies.

00:01:30.590 --> 00:01:33.250
No participant can transfer all risk to another party,

00:01:33.250 --> 00:01:36.470
and no single participant can manage the entire lifecycle alone.
